> Here is a report I am finishing:
> ===
> The Macroeconomic Case for Adaptation Investment
> Grantham Research Institute on Climate Change and the Environment
> London School of Economics
> Key Messages
> Adaptation investments can yield substantial returns, particularly considering the broad co-benefits and opportunities created, although economic returns often surpass financial ones.
> Government support is often needed to resolve market failures and achieve full adaptation benefits.
> Investing in resilience can address systemic risks and help achieve national development plans.
> Adaptation projects can unlock new international funding, including concessionary finance, technical assistance, and capacity building.
> Adaptation investments must be tailored to specific contexts but offer significant benefits on a national scale, supporting national economic objectives and enhancing fiscal health.
> There are abundant opportunities for adaptation, decarbonization, and development to effectively work together, by overcoming challenges related to coordination and market dynamics, and thereby crowd in private finance.
> Chapter 1: Introduction
> 
> In recent years, the world has witnessed a rapid increase in the frequency and intensity of extreme weather events. From devastating hurricanes and floods to prolonged droughts and wildfires, these events have wreaked havoc across the globe, affecting millions of lives and livelihoods. Such localised disasters can have far-reaching macroeconomic consequences, undermining national economic growth and upending government budgets. Climate impacts can ripple through national economies, exacerbating poverty and inequality, while stalling development efforts.
> 
> Climate change doesn’t just threaten current economic stability; it poses measurable risks to government finances by increasing public expenditures for disaster relief and recovery efforts, while simultaneously reducing revenue streams and potentially lowering sovereign credit ratings. This precarious fiscal situation highlights the urgent need for robust measures to mitigate these growing threats.
> 
> Fortunately, strategic investments in adaptation and resilience offer significant opportunities for managing these risks. By implementing adaptive measures, countries can significantly reduce the potential damages associated with climate change. Several National Adaptation Plans (NAPs) already illustrate innovative solutions being employed across different regions to bolster resilience. From infrastructure modifications to ecosystem-based approaches, these examples underscore that proactive adaptation is both feasible and effective.
> 
> Studies consistently demonstrate high rates of return on investments in adaptation, particularly in terms of risk reduction. Beyond reducing vulnerabilities, these investments frequently yield substantial co-benefits, such as support for economic development and provision of social and environmental advantages—a concept often termed the “triple dividend” of adaptation.
> What We Do in This Report
> This report aims to conduct a global review and synthesis of existing evidence to assess the economic costs of inaction and the benefits of adaptation investments. By focusing on macroeconomic and fiscal outcomes at the national level, we highlight the critical importance of proactive, public measures to address climate impacts.
> 
> We present evidence from three core elements to comprehensively assess the economic costs of inaction and the benefits of adaptation investments:
> Meta-Synthesis: We conduct a global review and synthesis of existing literature that examines macroeconomic and fiscal outcomes related to climate inaction and adaptation. This includes quantitative evidence of impacts from diverse regions, aiming to provide a holistic view of the economic landscape worldwide.
> Targeted Review of Select Studies: In areas where there is not yet a large body of evidence, we perform a targeted review of select studies to bridge gaps in knowledge and to illuminate emerging insights in adaptation investments.
> Case Studies Development: We analyze adaptation outcomes in six countries—the United Kingdom, Rwanda, Kenya, Nigeria, Brazil, and Bangladesh. These case studies focus on success factors, financing mechanisms, and national-scale benefits. They offer pivotal insights into the importance of government actions, providing comparative assessments of the economic outcomes related to investment or lack thereof in adaptation and resilience.
> 
> By concentrating on these three elements, we aim to furnish Finance Ministries with actionable insights that integrate adaptation into fiscal planning. The report underscores the critical importance of proactive, public measures in addressing climate impacts and highlights how adaptation can unlock international funding and bolster national development objectives.
> 
> Our results are all based on the existing evidence base in the academic and grey literature. The data we have collected draws upon evaluation studies of adaptation investments, modeling studies of future risks and adaptation potential, and econometric statistical studies of current impacts. These each bring important evidence to the table. Econometric studies, in particular, provide scientific results that emerge from– in many cases– very large datasets, allowing outcomes to be studied in the real world. Studies that use macroeconomic models are also important for looking decades ahead, and can reflect the potentially transformative effects of adaptation. A key challenge with such studies, however, is that they represent ex ante assumptions, and their results can diverge significantly from ex post evaluations of observed impacts and adaptation benefits.
> 
> We combine a review of 116 studies with an artificial intelligence methodology used to evaluate over 40,000 additional studies. Combined, we extract quantitative estimates of macroeconomic and fiscal risks and the potential for adaptation from 286 studies, resulting in over 6,500 distinct estimates.
> 
> While the available evidence on macroeconomic risks has evolved rapidly in recent years, our knowledge of the socioeconomic consequences of climate change and the potential for adaptation remains highly uncertain, sorely incomplete, and noticeably biased toward certain countries.
> 
> Despite this, the evidence on climate impacts and adaptation is unequivocal and compelling, highlighting the urgent need for concrete responses. Socioeconomic impacts of climate change will be highly disruptive across the globe, from the individual to the macroeconomic levels. Existing adaptation actions offer large benefits across a wide range of sectors. The presence of uncertainty in this context motivates strong, precautionary action, to avoid the worst outcomes. The available evidence provides a strong basis for action today, because every additional year that either an energy transition or a large-scale adaptation investment is delayed will both result in more losses and presage even greater ones.
> 
> Recognizing the need for actionable insights, this report specifically targets Ministries of Finance, which oversee public financing exceeding $30 trillion per annum globally, along with other economic decision-makers. Ministries of Finance play a crucial role in shaping national economic policies and resource allocation, making them pivotal in addressing climate change. Their responsibilities encompass crafting fiscal policies that dictate where public funds are directed, as well as managing national budgets to prioritize investments in climate adaptation and resilience initiatives.
> 
> By integrating adaptation strategies into budgetary frameworks, Ministries of Finance can promote economic stability against climate-related shocks, ultimately safeguarding public finances while contributing to national development objectives. Our findings emphasize the significant advantages of this approach, empowering Ministries of Finance with actionable insights to champion effective climate action.
> Overview of our results
> 
> We present a high-level overview of our report’s findings, which focus on projections for 2050 under various “business-as-usual” scenarios, as reflected in existing literature. We use the year 2050 because it represents the middle of the lifespan of many infrastructure adaptations that can be initiated today. Where possible, we translate results according to a baseline future climate scenario which combines the baseline, high-emissions climate projections from the IPCC with estimates of the effects of tipping points from Dietz et al. (2021) (see appendix A.1).
> 
> Our baseline, high-emissions scenario is based on the IPCC’s Shared Socioeconomic Pathway SSP3-7.0. This scenario represents a divided world
> 
> Under a high-emissions baseline scenario, the world is projected to warm globally by 2.5 °C [2.2 – 2.8 °C] above pre-industrial levels by 2050 and 3.9 °C [3.5 – 4.3 °C] by 2100. This high emissions scenario results in much higher damages than the low emissions SSP1-2.6 scenario, which has 2 °C [1.8 – 2.3 °C] warming by 2050. These warming levels are higher than the results from the IPCC for the same scenarios because of the effect of tipping points missing from the original analyses.
> Unless emissions are rapidly reduced, the next 25 years are projected to produce 50% as much additional warming, for the average person, as the past 250 years. The average person lives in a country that is about 2 °C warmer in 2025 than it was under pre-industrial conditions, and this could increase to about 3 °C in 2050. This additional warming will come with a variety of regional and global tipping points exacerbating the climate problem. Across the globe, such rapid warming will drive massive heat-related mortality, wide-spread labour productivity loss, crop failures, flooding, droughts, ecosystem disruption, and many other direct impacts. How countries respond to these impacts could amplify or avoid their worst consequences.
> 
> As the global climate crisis intensifies, the need for investment in adaptation strategies has become more pressing than ever. Severe losses lie ahead; based on a synthesis of 85 econometric models, some nations in sub-Saharan Africa and Southeast Asia could face GDP losses in 2050 exceeding 20% due to temperature shocks alone, while total welfare losses are expected to be far higher. Alarmingly, many least developed countries are already experiencing the negative impacts of climate change, with estimates suggesting they could be 10% poorer today as a result of current warming.
> 
> Synthesising estimates from 8 studies, we find that labour productivity loss is projected to exceed 5% in regions like Africa and Asia, while four studies on TFP growth suggest that it could be reduced by up to 0.3 percentage points per year. These have the potential to drive long-term economic stagnation in some regions.
> 
> The fiscal landscape is increasingly intertwined with the challenges of climate change, as impacts manifest through sovereign credit ratings and government financial metrics. As climate risks evolve, they exert an influence on sovereign creditworthiness, directly affecting borrowing costs and fiscal stability. Both emerging and developed economies are vulnerable, facing downgrades and elevated risk premia as climate shocks increase (Volz et al., 2020; Zenios, 2022, Klusak et al. 2023). For example, the 2022 floods in Pakistan affected 33 million people, caused USD 30 billion in damages, and led Fitch to downgrade the country’s credit rating, citing liquidity pressures exacerbated by the disaster (Fitch, 2022). Forward-looking models suggest that by 2030, nearly 60 countries could face average sovereign downgrades of 0.7 notches, rising to over 2 notches by 2100 (Klusak et al., 2023).
> 
> Beyond sovereign credit concerns, climate change is expected to drive up government expenditures while eroding revenues. There is considerable variability in expecture impacts across regions. While the Australian Government (2023) estimates an increase in public expenditures of 33% per degree warming for Australia, Leppänen et al. (2015) estimate a decrease of 1.5 to 2.6% per degree of warming for Russia. Even so, global trends indicate that extreme climate events lead to higher expenditure-to-GDP ratios and diminished revenue ratios, constraining fiscal balances (Akaypi et al., 2025). The fiscal impacts are compounded by a vicious cycle where vulnerable economies face escalating borrowing costs, hindering their ability to finance crucial climate adaptation measures.
> 
> However, there is a ray of hope in the form of smart investment in adaptation. We collect adaptation costs, benefits, and economic returns from 75 studies, and are able to combine results from 22 studies into three consistent metrics of the benefits of adaptation: Adaptation ratios (e.g., World Bank 2024), Economic benefit-cost ratios (e.g., World Economic Forum 2024), and Economic internal rates of return (e.g., World Resources Institute 2025).
> 
> Drawing on results from 14 studies, we find that adaptation actions deliver a median economic benefit-cost ratio of at least 4 to 1, and the median internal rate of return is an impressive 25%. This makes adaptation not just a necessary response but one of the best economic investments available, capable of halving the projected impacts of climate change on economies.
> 
> Proactive action on the part of governments, including MoF, can both reduce these risks and pave the way to concessionary funding. As a national average, adaptation financing that supports locally-targeted adaptation can reduce risks by 38 - 72% (World Bank 2024). The rise in borrowing costs can be reduced or reversed through investment in resilience with every 1% change in resilience reducing the change in borrowing costs by 0.75% in developing countries (IMF 2020).
> 
> Overall, these findings underscore the critical role of proactive adaptation strategies in mitigating both immediate and long-term economic and welfare losses, highlighting the importance of investing in resilience to safeguard vulnerable economies against the escalating risks of climate change.
> 
