> 
> At the same time, there are significant research gaps. We find that tipping points and warming threshold effects in the economy are almost entirely absent in the risk analyses. Mid- and late-century impacts are likely underestimated, as a result. Across the risks we consider, there is little evidence for unemployment, capital depreciation loss, and changing interest rates. None of the fiscal outcomes (expenditures, revenues, and debt) were conducive to a synthesis. Although adaptation was accounted for in some way in many of the contributing studies, the inclusion of adaptation was irregular and incomplete.
> 
> Across studies that provide evidence of the effectiveness of adaptation, there is a lack of a macro-level perspective. Most adaptation evidence (both ex post and ex ante) are at the project level. The coverage of adaptation benefits is varied, with some studies considering only avoided losses, studies using multiple incompatible definitions of non-market benefits, and studies generally not distinguishing benefits that can accrue to governments. In general, cost recovery and institutional issues are not explored. Adaptation costs are also evaluated in different ways, but generally do not include real-world frictions and transition costs. All of these topics require much more research to form a coherent picture.
> A framework for understanding climate risks and potential for adaptation investment
> This section provides a general framework to aid in understanding and interpreting the quantitative results presented in this report, while also pointing toward how these can be translated into effective action.
> 
> Climate risks are multifaceted, emerging from both acute shocks and gradual trends and cascading and simultaneous events. Realised impacts come from the interaction between hazards, population and capital exposed, and multiple forms of socioeconomic vulnerability. While climate change continues to disrupt the world, economies will continue to evolve.
> 
> Adaptation actions include household and workplace cooling, irrigation and soil management, coastal defenses, and managed retreat. All of these require significant planning and outlays of capital, and many adaptation choices have consequences far beyond the locales where they are implemented.
> 
> We are mainly concerned with policy-driven adaptation, but this is only part of the necessary action. Along with any public action, individuals and firms will make adaptation investments for their private benefit. This is called “autonomous adaptation.” Rising incomes in many regions will also confer significant benefits, in the form of reduced vulnerability and greater adaptive capacity. However, the universal scale of impacts means that even when private action is possible, it will often be overpriced and under-invested. Policy-driven adaptation investment is crucial to achieve economic goals.
> 
> One of those economic goals is to take advantage of all of the actions that will provide more benefit than they will cost to implement. This is a basic economic tenant: both economic growth and the greatest good can often be pursued just by identifying actions where benefits exceed costs.
> 
> The benefits of interest are generally not restricted to GDP growth. Many climate impacts reduce welfare more than they reduce economic production. For example, the heightened risk of death from heat waves represents a significant loss of welfare, even if the elderly who are most at risk do not directly contribute to the economy. Because we include these “non-market” risks and the non-market benefits of adaptation, Ministries of Finance can only expect a portion of the benefits to accrue to their balance sheets. The value added to people’s lives is real, nonetheless, and creative policies can capture part of this value in monetary terms and still leave people better off.
> 
> Moreover, climate damages as a percentage of global GDP are lowest when adaptation and mitigation strategies are combined and implemented simultaneously (Agrawala et al., 2010). 
> 
> The final chapter of this report develops a framework for translating the quantitative results in this report into potential actions, but the basic principles of that framework are well-known:
> 
> First, adaptation should be driven by robust policy frameworks that prioritize actions with the greatest potential impact. It involves developing strategic pathways that integrate adaptation into broader development and economic plans.
> 
> Second, the inherent uncertainties in climate projections necessitate decision-making approaches that are flexible, adaptive, and precautionary. Policies must be robust under a wide range of possible future scenarios and should incorporate adaptive management techniques to adjust actions as new information becomes available.
> 
> Third, adaptation strategies must be tailored to specific local contexts, accounting for unique geographic, cultural, and economic characteristics. Engaging local communities and stakeholders in the planning and implementation processes ensures that solutions are relevant, effective, and supported by those directly affected.
> 
> The mispricing of climate risk and adaptation creates a vicious cycle. Vulnerable economies are penalized with higher borrowing costs, which constrain their ability to finance adaptation, leaving them more exposed to the next shock. This cycle disproportionately affects low- and middle-income countries. Breaking it requires policy and market reforms that recognize adaptation as a credit-enhancing factor. Priority measures include improved disclosure and integration of climate risks into debt sustainability analyses, the development of innovative financial instruments such as resilience bonds and contingent credit lines, and stronger support from multilateral development banks (ADB, 2019; EBRD, 2019).
> Chapter 2: Macroeconomic and Fiscal Risks
> Macroeconomic risks refer to the potential threats to a country’s economic stability and growth. Key indicators such as Gross Domestic Product (GDP) per capita growth rate and GDP-equivalent welfare loss serve as primary metrics for assessing economic performance and societal well-being. A decline in GDP per capita signals stagnation, while welfare loss quantifies the broader impacts of economic stressors, highlighting the relationship between economic health and quality of life.
> 
> In addition to these primary indicators, secondary measures like total factor productivity and labour productivity shed light on the productive foundation of the economy. High productivity levels indicate a robust economy, while declines suggest underlying problems. Other important factors include capital depreciation, which affects investment capacity, as well as interest rates, inflation, and unemployment, all of which can significantly influence economic growth and public finances.
> 
> Fiscal risks encompass the potential challenges that can undermine a government’s financial stability and its ability to fund public services. Understanding these risks is essential for maintaining fiscal health and ensuring sustainable economic growth.
> 
> Primary indicators, such as public expenditures, revenues,  borrowing costs, and debt, are crucial for assessing a government’s financial dynamics. High borrowing costs, influenced by sovereign credit ratings, can strain public finances and limit a government’s ability to finance essential services or infrastructure projects. Furthermore, the growth of sovereign debt can signal potential future challenges related to repayment and fiscal discipline.
> 
> It is also important to consider assets at risk from physical impacts, particularly in the context of climate change. Such risks can lead to unexpected expenditures or losses, further complicating a government’s fiscal landscape, and these already exist as contingent liabilities.
> 
> Macroeconomic outcomes such as GDP loss and the other items above represent only part of the damaging consequences of climate change. Climate change is expected to undermine health and human capital, reduce ecosystems stability and natural productivity, and disrupt communities and cultures. GDP and macroeconomic indicators do not capture many of these effects. Alternatives, such as inclusive wealth accounting and welfare calculations, provide a more comprehensive picture but no one measure is sufficient for basing decisions on.
> 
> At the same time, GDP loss is a crucially important indicator for key processes. First, as a measure of consumption and exports, GDP is a strong predictor of government revenues. While the elasticity of tax revenue varies depending on the structure of the economy in a country and its tax policies, declines in GDP usually result in corresponding declines in tax revenue. Second, while consumption is a poor predictor of well-being at higher income levels, it is an important input to it at lower levels of income. For poorer countries, GDP correlates with life expectancy and many other indicators of well-being.
> Impacts on Gross Domestic Product
> Using a subset of the studies concerned with top-down macroeconomic impacts, we look at impacts of climate change on GDP at the national level. These results are based on a meta-analysis of 85 econometric models from 15 macroeconomic studies of the impacts of temperature on GDP growth. The meta-analysis method combines Monte Carlo draws from each study, according to how well it predicts future GDP growth estimates beyond the period on which each model was calibrated (see appendix A.2). We also include the effects of tipping points on global temperatures (Dietz et al. 2021), market losses from sea-level rise and storm surge (Depsky et al. 2023), and an estimate of the effects of trade spillovers (Rising 2023) .
> As a meta-analysis of macro-econometric results, several important features are missing, generally due to the limitations of current econometric methods. Importantly, these models do not account for non-market welfare losses, such as impacts on health and the biosphere, which are expected to be large; this is missing because it is outside the intended scope of these analyses. Total economic damages (including market and non-market losses) are expected to be considerably higher than these estimated market losses. In addition, our synthesis of the results misses (1) impacts that are not strongly driven by temperature shocks, such as slow onset effects, water supply and demand impacts, and other extreme events, such as surface and river floods, droughts, and wildfire; (2) regional-scale tipping points such as AMOC and sea-level rise tipping points; and (3) future adaptation and the results of broader changes in the economy. These aspects are each included in some form in some macroeconometric studies, but we do not project these aspects of the models in our reanalysis.
> On average, climate change lowers GDP by 8% in 2050. Using this evidence base, we find that under the high emissions scenario, the average person lives in a country that is projected to be 8% [3 - 15%] poorer in 2050 than it would have been without climate change. The greatest losses are across low-income countries and across the tropics. In particular, sub-Saharan Africa and Southeast Asia face the largest losses, with some countries expected to lose 20% of their GDP due to climate change by 2050.
> Some individual study estimates are significantly higher such as those used by the Network for Greening the Financial System (NGFS), which relies on Kotz et al. (2024a), and Bilal & Känzig (2025), primarily due to longer persistence of impacts and accounting for the role of global-scale temperatures. Our estimates are a synthesis of 85 estimates and hence represent an average, which is likely to change over time as the representation of extreme events and other factors improve. In particular, the lack of extreme events suggests that this may be a significant underestimate.
> Percentage losses in GDP in 2050 under High Emissions
> 
> Figure 1. Colours and labels show the loss in GDP due to warming from 1960 to 2050, under an high-emissions baseline scenario, as a percent of each country’s counterfactual GDP without climate change. Countries for which the interquartile range of uncertainty crosses 0 are shown with a diagonal pattern and faded labels. Not all countries are labelled, to reduce visual clutter.
> 
>  
> Poor countries are hit the hardest. There is a strong income gradient in these losses, with climate change reinforcing existing inequalities. Least developed countries are projected to be already 10% [5 – 15%] poorer due to climate change today, 14% in 2050, and 20% by 2075. Because of the spatial inequality of these impacts, global GDP is projected to be only 5% lower in 2050, nearly half of the average person impact..
