> Figure 5.. Expected impact on labour productivity and total factor productivity growth, due to climate change, from a synthesis of studies. Studies used different scenarios, but only scenarios that can be considered middle-of-the-road, current policies, or high-emissions scenarios are included. Interquartile ranges (error bars) cover both within-study and across-study uncertainty.
> 
> Other macroeconomic outcomes
> Total factor productivity (TFP) growth is expected to be lowered by climate change. This is based on combining the results of three econometric studies, reporting the pooled synthesis as described in appendix A.4. These include autonomous adaptation. The largest expected reductions in growth are in Africa, at 0.05 percentage points lower per year. There is considerable uncertainty around these numbers, however, with a 1-in-4 chance that TFP is 0.3 percentage points lower per year across much of the world, which would result in a 7% loss in TFP by 2050.
> 
> These indicators of productivity have consequences for capital investment. Greater economic activity drives greater investment into produced capital, and climate impacts can undermine this investment process. Reduced investment also results in a lower productive capital base, driving a feedback loop that can result in an even greater gap in economic potential.
> 
> Effects of droughts and higher temperatures are more significant for the economy when the agricultural sector constitutes a relevant share of the country's economic activities. 
>  
> Countries with a larger agricultural sector are more vulnerable to harsh droughts than countries that don't rely on these weather-exposed activities and are exposed to higher temperatures (Akyapi et al., 2025). The CCDRs from the World Bank highlight that the higher vulnerability of countries depends on their reliance on agriculture for employment and income in dry/hot scenarios (World Bank, 2024d).  
>  
> In Kenya, the main driver of GDP loss in a dry/hot scenario is crop output reduction and erosion caused by higher temperatures (World Bank, 2023e). In Morocco, water yield declines will cause a reduction of 2.8-7.1% of GDP due to reduced agricultural productivity caused by water shortage (Pérez-Cutillas, 2024). In Greece, direct economic losses related to agricultural output decline are between 437 million and 1 billion euros per year due to extreme temperature events (Georgopoulou et al., 2024). 
> 
> Acute shocks in temperatures have a short-term effect on inflation, but not in the long-term. Higher temperatures increase inflation persistently for up to 12 months in both higher and lower income countries (Qi et al., 2025). An increase of 1% in temperature leads to an increase in the inflation rate of 0.0189%, according to econometric analysis based on historical data. However, a single shock does not have persistence over more than one year (Lo et al., 2023). A study by the Network for Greening the Financial System (2025) suggests that in the long-term there can even be deflation due to a recovery in production. 
> 
> Nevertheless, constant weather shocks could cause persistent upwards pressures on inflation every year. In this sense, global inflation per year will be expected to increase by 1.79 percentage points under a RCP8.5 scenario by 2035 (Kotz et al., 2024b).
> 
> 
> 
> Projected increases in Inflation Rates in 2050
> 
> Figure 6. Increases in inflation rates, driven by physical and transition risks. Estimates combine Kotz et al. (2024b) and Boirard et al. (2022) as described in appendix A.4.
> 
>  
> Evidence suggests that multiple mechanisms drive this price increase, with food prices being the primary factor pushing this macroeconomic indicator upward. Agricultural prices increased in 121 countries when there was an increase in temperature (Yusifzada, 2024). In fact, food inflation has been found to be higher than headline inflation when there are climatic shocks. Another mechanism is related to interest rates. With extreme weather events, there is economic contraction and therefore central banks reduce interest rates, which in turn increases inflation (Qi et al., 2025). A shortage in energy supply due to climatic shocks could also raise prices (Li et al., 2023). The impact of weather shocks on supply chains and therefore on inflation has been found to be relevant in some countries, but not all (Cevik et al., 2024). 
> 
> Global warming significantly impacts unemployment rates. As temperatures rise, inflation and disruptions in economic activity and agricultural output can lead to declining asset values and business activities, which can increase unemployment due to deflationary pressures. Using econometric methods, Liu & Lin (2023) find that countries located between 20 and 40 degrees latitude experience higher unemployment linked to global warming, while regions above 40 degrees may see a reduction in unemployment. Furthermore, the effects of climate change vary across economic contexts, with middle-income countries bearing a greater burden and male unemployment being more affected than female unemployment. Long-term implications such as decreased labor productivity and increased labor costs in certain sectors further complicate this dynamic. Consequently, it is essential for policymakers to develop adaptive economic policies that address these climate-induced changes in unemployment dynamics to ensure resilient financial and labor systems.
> 
> 
> Change in unemployment rate in 2050
> 
> Figure 7. Changes in unemployment rate driven by warming under an SSP3-7.0 scenario with tipping points, projected using estimates from Liu & Lin 2023.
> 
> 
> Climate impacts can drive increases in inequality. Since climate change disproportionately affects vulnerable populations, disrupting livelihoods. Emmerling et al. (2024) extend an ensemble of eight large-scale integrated assessment models with income groups to study this effect. Under a scenario without climate policy, the Gini coefficient, as an index of inequality, is projected to rise by 2050. As climate damages amplify existing disparities, they can contribute to greater economic instability and social unrest. Conversely, implementing policies aligned with the Paris Agreement, and redistributing the carbon revenues from those policies on an equal per-capita basis (EPC) more than counteracts these inequality increases. This approach transforms potential risks into benefits by using redistribution as an adaptation mechanism.
> Change in the Gini index in 2050, with and without redistributive policies
> Figure 8. Estimates for 2050 under the No Climate Policy and Paris with equal per capita redistribution scheme, from a model intercomparison by Emmerling et al. (2024).
> 
> Sovereign credit ratings
> Sovereign creditworthiness is increasingly shaped by the impacts of climate change. A sovereign’s credit rating influences its cost of borrowing, with direct implications for both public and private financing. Evidence shows that physical climate risks are already translating into sovereign downgrades and higher borrowing costs, particularly for emerging and developing economies, while the benefits of adaptation remain under-recognized (Volz et al., 2020; Zenios, 2022).
> 
> ​​Empirical research demonstrates that countries exposed to climate shocks face higher risk premia and increased default probabilities. For example, the 2022 floods in Pakistan affected 33 million people, caused USD 30 billion in damages, and led Fitch to downgrade the country’s credit rating, citing liquidity pressures exacerbated by the disaster (Fitch, 2022). Forward-looking models suggest that by 2030, nearly 60 countries could face average sovereign downgrades of 0.7 notches, rising to over 2 notches by 2100 (Klusak et al., 2023).
> 
> Recent downgrade data illustrates this trajectory. We apply an existing analysis to evaluate these impacts and find that all regions of the world are at risk of downgrades (Klusak et al. 2023). In contrast to our macroeconomic results, we also find that the greatest impacts of climate change on sovereign credit ratings fall on upper middle income and high-income countries, across all regions. This is likely driven by the greater potential reductions in credit ratings for higher income countries. Some highly vulnerable countries do not have a credit rating.
> 
> At the same time, the risk to credit worthiness for poorer countries is already present, with climate-vulnerable countries facing a measurable risk premium in their debt markets, which reflects both their heightened exposure to extreme events and their limited fiscal capacity to respond (Kling et al., 2018; Cevik & Jalles, 2021). 
> 
> Sovereign credit downgrading across regions and income groups in 2050
> 
> Figure 9. Impact of climate change on sovereign credit ratings, evaluated under a high-emissions scenario by Klusak et al. (2023), and adjusted to represent 2050. Number labels report the number of countries available in each region-income group from Klusak et al.
> 
> 
> Despite these risks, adaptation efforts are rarely reflected in credit assessments. Large-scale investments in resilient infrastructure, early warning systems, and disaster risk finance can materially reduce fiscal vulnerability. However, credit rating agencies typically acknowledge such measures only when they yield immediate fiscal benefits (Andersen et al., 2025). This results in systematic undervaluation of adaptation, even though empirical work shows that adaptive capacity is a significant determinant of sovereign credit outcomes (De Moor et al., 2018; Cevik & Jalles, 2022). The downgrades illustrated in Figures 1 and 2 highlight what is at stake: without adaptation, countries in climate-vulnerable regions face mounting fiscal stress and escalating borrowing costs.
> 
> The mispricing of climate risk and adaptation creates a vicious cycle. Vulnerable economies are penalized with higher borrowing costs, which constrain their ability to finance adaptation, leaving them more exposed to the next shock. As Figures 1 and 2 demonstrate, this cycle disproportionately affects low- and middle-income countries. Breaking it requires policy and market reforms that recognize adaptation as a credit-enhancing factor. Priority measures include improved disclosure and integration of climate risks into debt sustainability analyses, the development of innovative financial instruments such as resilience bonds and contingent credit lines, and stronger support from multilateral development banks (ADB, 2019; EBRD, 2019).
> 
> Sovereign downgrades linked to climate impacts are no longer hypothetical—they are observable realities. Unless adaptation is systematically integrated into sovereign credit assessments, many vulnerable economies risk being trapped in a downward spiral of downgrades and rising debt costs. Embedding adaptation into financial analysis is therefore not only an environmental imperative but also a fiscal one (Klusak et al., 2023; Boston Consulting Group & GRP, 2022).
> Other fiscal impacts
> Government expenditure is expected to increase and government revenue is expected to decrease with climatic shocks. Nevertheless, changes in public debt are uncertain as climate change may downgrade countries' credit ratings. 
> 
> The effect of climate change on public expenditures shows a wide variation. While the Australian Government (2023) estimates an increase in public expenditures of 33% per degree warming for Australia, Leppänen et al. (2015) estimate a decrease of 1.5 to 2.6% per degree of warming for Russia.
>  
> At a global level, harsh droughts lead to a significant increase of 0.11 percentage points in the government expenditure-to-GDP ratio, and an increase in the occurrence of extreme hot days reduces the government revenue-to-GDP ratio by 0.14 percentage points (Akaypi et al., 2025). In Australia, the increased frequency and severity of natural disasters are expected to increase expenditure by 3 to 3.6 times over the following 40 years (Australian Government, 2023). In the US, the public expenditure increase caused by extreme hot days raises the social cost of carbon by 23-33% (Barrage, 2024). 
> 
> Nevertheless, evidence of the effect of climatic shocks on public debt is not concluding. Two econometric studies based on historical data find that debt does not increase significantly after climatic shocks. According to a study from Akaypi et al. (2025), extreme temperatures or droughts do not have a significant impact on public debt at a global level. The authors find this result hard to interpret and do not provide any explanation for this finding.  A study in the US found that public debt was reduced 10 years following hurricane exposure. The main reason is that interest rates increased by 1% after hurricane exposure, due to the fact that these natural disasters increased the default risk by 0.1 percentage points. As a result, public debt did not increase, but government expenditure was reduced (Jerch et al., 2023).These findings suggest that governments might address fiscal deficits by reducing expenditure rather than relying solely on increased borrowing.
> 
> Other studies have used modeling approaches to understand debt impacts. Gagliardi et al. (2022) perform a stylised analysis of the fiscal impact of natural disasters, finding increases between 0.7 pp and 4.5 pp across EU countries under a 1.5 °C scenario and from 0.9 to 5.2 pp under a 2 °C scenario. Lamperti et al. (2019) use an agent-based model to evaluate the effects of capital and labour damages and find that the Debt-to-GDP ratio increases by up to 94% on average when climate change driven impacts on both are considered.
> 
> Interest rates are expected to increase annual interest payments on sovereign debt by US$45-67 billion under the RCP2.6 scenario and US$135-203 billion under the RCP8.5 scenario. 
>  
> The metric of fiscal (primary) balance seems ripe for analysis: climate change is likely to both reduce revenues and increase expenditures for governments, and the effect on their combination may be more tractable than either individually. However, evidence of fiscal balance is very sparse. Within our review, two papers provided this outcome: a stylised analysis of fiscal balance for EU countries due to natural disasters (Gagliardi et al.) and an analysis of optimal fiscal rules for Pacific Islands (Nakatani 2021).
> 
> 
> Box 1. Fiscal Benefits of Investing in Integrated Water Management to Reduce Flood Risk: Nigeria Case Study
> 
> Floods are Nigeria’s costliest and most frequent natural hazard. The 2022 floods alone caused an estimated US$6.68 billion in direct damages and up to US$9.1 billion in broader economic losses, straining federal and state budgets through emergency relief, rehabilitation of transport and energy assets, and expanded social protection needs (World Bank, 2023). Integrated water resources management (IWRM)—which coordinates land, water, and risk across basins and sectors, blending gray and nature-based infrastructure with data, regulation, and finance—offers a fiscally efficient path to reduce these losses. Global and regional evidence indicates benefit-cost ratios around 4:1 for resilient infrastructure and early-warning-enabled preparedness, with strong co-benefits for jobs, health, and productivity (Hallegatte et al., 2019). Applying IWRM in Nigeria’s priority basins, particularly the Niger–Benue, can meaningfully lower disaster spending, protect revenues, and crowd in private investment.
> 1.   Floods as a Structural Fiscal Risk
> Nigeria’s exposure to flood risk is rising due to rapid urbanization, watershed degradation, silted channels, and climate-intensified extreme rainfall. In 2018, coastal degradation, dominated by flooding and erosion, in just three states—Lagos, Delta, and Cross River—cost an estimated US$9.7 billion, equivalent to approximately 2.4% of national GDP, underscoring large, recurring welfare and fiscal impacts from flood-related service disruptions and health burdens (World Bank, 2020). The 2022 floods affected 64% of households nationally, with especially severe impacts on non-farm enterprises, 91% of which reported adverse effects (UNDP & NBS, 2023). These damages drove tax base erosion and higher emergency outlays. Federal estimates put economy-wide losses near US$9.1 billion, while rapid assessments reported US$6.68 billion in direct damages to housing, transport, agriculture, and public assets—costs that risk ultimately cascading to public budgets.
> 2.   Integrated Water Resources Management in Practice
> Integrated water resources management (IWRM) is a basin-scale approach that aligns water allocation, land use, flood protection, water quality, drought management, and ecosystem services under one governance and investment plan. It is grounded in the Dublin-Rio principles and embedded in Nigeria’s water governance through the Nigeria Integrated Water Resources Management Commission (NIWRMC) and River Basin Development Authorities—though implementation remains uneven (NIWRMC, 2021). In practice, a Nigerian IWRM package for flood risk reduction typically combines upstream and peri-urban nature-based solutions such as watershed reforestation and floodplain restoration, targeted gray works like dredging and urban drainage upgrades, real-time hydromet networks and flood early warning systems, as well as policy reforms including zoning, building control, and risk-layered financing (World Resources Institute, 2022).
> 3.   The Fiscal Case for IWRM
> The fiscal rationale for IWRM rests on several pillars. First, avoided damages to public infrastructure and services are significant. Evidence from the World Bank’s Lifelines study shows that every dollar invested in resilient infrastructure yields about four dollars in benefits, mainly by reducing repair costs and downtime (Hallegatte et al., 2019). IWRM also lowers emergency and social protection outlays. Early-warning-enabled preparedness reduces casualties and asset losses, with high benefit-cost ratios documented in low- and middle-income countries (UNDRR, 2019). By keeping firms operating and supply chains intact, IWRM also protects Nigeria’s tax base and economic output. For instance, in 2018 alone, coastal flooding and erosion eroded an estimated 2.4% of GDP in just three states (World Bank, 2020). Furthermore, IWRM interventions such as wetland restoration improve water quality and reduce the burden of water-borne diseases, lowering public health expenditure (World Bank, 2020). Finally, embedding resilience into infrastructure projects reduces risk perceptions and attracts private investment; while resilience features typically add 1–3% to upfront costs, they yield returns of around 4:1 and improve credit terms (World Bank, 2019).
> 4.   Illustrative Fiscal Benefits
> A hypothetical US$500 million IWRM flood-resilience program in the Niger–Benue basin, combining upstream nature-based solutions, urban drainage upgrades, and forecasting systems, could generate benefits worth around US$2 billion based on average BCRs. If early warning systems were to reduce just 10% of the US$6.68 billion in damages recorded in the 2022 floods, this would translate into approximately US$668 million in avoided losses in a single severe season (World Bank, 2023). In Lagos, Delta, and Cross River states, reducing coastal flood and erosion costs by 15–20% could protect between US$1.5 and 1.9 billion annually (World Bank, 2020). These back-of-the-envelope estimates suggest that IWRM investments can pay back within a single political cycle.
> 5.   Real-World IWRM Experiences in Nigeria
> Nigeria’s experience with IWRM illustrates both successes and persistent challenges. The Ibadan Urban Flood Management Project (IUFMP), launched after the devastating 2011 floods, represents a hybrid model that combined basin-scale planning, infrastructure investments, and an early warning system. By 2024, the project had rehabilitated 37 hydraulic structures, completed dam safety works, dredged 431 kilometers of rivers and drains, and installed early warning systems benefiting over 2.1 million residents. These investments, funded by a US$200 million World Bank credit, demonstrated how integrated planning reduces emergency repair costs, though sustainability hinges on formal adoption of masterplans and adequate operations and maintenance budgets (World Bank, 2024a).
> In northern Nigeria, the Komadugu–Yobe Basin Trust Fund was established by six riparian states and the federal government with AfDB support to coordinate dam operations at Tiga and Challawa Gorge. The goal was to restore downstream flows to the Hadejia–Nguru wetlands while reducing destructive floods. Benefits included improved fisheries, farming opportunities, and reduced conflicts between irrigation and downstream users. However, institutional complexity and the lack of enforceable operating rules have limited its full effectiveness (AfDB, 2015).
> The Nigeria Erosion and Watershed Management Project (NEWMAP), implemented across more than 20 states, rehabilitated degraded watersheds using bioengineering and drainage improvements. NEWMAP generated a net present value of US$317 million with a benefit-cost ratio of 1.4 and an internal rate of return of 15%. These results confirm that upstream rehabilitation reduces sedimentation, prevents infrastructure damage, and is fiscally cheaper than repeated dredging or road repairs (World Bank, 2022).
> By contrast, the Oyan Dam along the Ogun River illustrates the risks of incomplete integration. Seasonal releases regularly inundate downstream communities in Lagos and Ogun States. While authorities issue early warnings and relocate households, limited transparency on reservoir operations, weak enforcement of zoning laws, and solid waste clogging drains mean that flood losses persist year after year. This example demonstrates that early warning systems, though valuable, cannot deliver fiscal savings unless paired with transparent dam management and urban planning reforms (NEMA, 2024).
> 
> Research gaps
> Only 3% of sources reviewed take into account tipping points. Mid- and late-century impacts are likely underestimated, as a result. Generally, effects that emerge from future thresholds are not represented in these results. Both statistical and macromodeling approaches assume that the basic structure of the economy, stability of the climate, and relationship between the economy and the climate are relatively unchanged in the future. However, the “Reasons for Concern” in recent IPCC reports has highlighted the near-term risks that some of these unknown thresholds pose.
> 
> The role of extreme events and precipitation in general is understudied. For example, only impacts driven by temperature change and sea-level rise (coastal inundation and storm surge) are included in the GDP results above. While there has been some work to incorporate hazards other than temperature into econometric analysis of GDP, the contributions of these other factors is found to be much smaller than the temperature effects (Waidelich et al. 2024). The minor role of these other factors is likely a limitation in the ways that extreme events have been represented, and more work is needed to improve these representations.
> 
> As shown in Figure 10, there is a strong bias in the data for large countries, like the United States and China. Macroeconomic and fiscal risks across much of the lower income world remain poorly studied.
> Availability of macroeconomic estimates and sources
> 
> 
> Figure 10. Global distribution of available macroeconomic estimates. Top: The number of estimates related to macroeconomic and fiscal risks, by country. For estimates applied uniformly to many countries, the estimate count is evenly divided across countries. Bottom: The number of sources providing country-specific values, by country.
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> Studies on unemployment unrelated to energy transition mitigation scenarios and opportunities are limited. Therefore, it is important to examine how high temperatures, weather shocks, and sea level rise impact unemployment at the national level.
> 
> The review identified only two studies that analyzed the impacts of climate change on total factor productivity. This variable is crucial for explaining long-term economic growth.
> 
> Studies related to capital loss are typically static in nature and show the accumulation of capital loss over time. Given that capital loss represents a reduction in a country's wealth, this usually has implications for consumption, productivity, and therefore welfare over time. Consequently, there are opportunities for new research to focus on the long-term welfare impacts of capital loss. Additionally, studies quantifying natural and human capital loss due to climate change are also limited.
> 
> The interaction between the downgrading of sovereign credit ratings, increased public expenditure, and decreased public revenue caused by climate change should be studied in greater detail. There is insufficient evidence of how countries will address fiscal deficits, for example, which budget categories could be affected by reduced government revenues.
> Chapter 3. The case for adaptation investment
> As the impacts of climate change continue to escalate, the imperative for strategic adaptation investment becomes ever more pressing for economic decision-makers, particularly within Ministries of Finance. The rationale for such investments extends beyond mere climate resilience; it encompasses a broader economic and social landscape that offers a multitude of dividends.
> 
> Investing in climate adaptation provides a critical opportunity to mitigate the adverse impacts of climate change before they manifest into more severe economic disruptions. Effective adaptation measures can prevent or reduce losses in crucial sectors, such as agriculture, infrastructure, and public health, thereby safeguarding national and local economies from unforeseen shocks.
> 
> At the heart of adaptation investment lies the concept of avoided impacts—an analytical framework that assesses the potential damages averted through proactive measures. By investing in adaptation today, economic decision-makers can diminish future vulnerabilities and stabilize financial climates, averting costs that would otherwise arise from inaction in the face of climate disruption.
> 
> Beyond avoided losses, adaptation investments yield a ‘triple dividend.’ First, they prevent loss and damages by protecting economic assets and communities. Second, they stimulate economic activities and innovations, often leading to job creation and increased productivity. Third, they deliver social and environmental co-benefits, such as improved public health and enhanced biodiversity, which further enhance the quality of life for communities.
> 
> Increasing resilience through adaptation investments also fosters a more sustainable and robust socioeconomic fabric. It empowers communities through capacity building, enhances adaptive governance, and builds societal cohesion by promoting shared responses to climate risks.
> Evidence of adaptation effectiveness
> We collect adaptation costs, benefits, and economic returns from 75 studies, and are able to combine results from 22 studies into three consistent metrics of the benefits of adaptation: adaptation ratios (World Bank 2024), benefit-cost ratios (World Economic Forum 2024), and economic internal rates of return (World Resources Institute 2025). The methodology is described in appendix A.5.
> Our approach focuses on the macroeconomic perspective for adaptation benefits. We synthesise results from multiple studies, and we infer metrics where the component outcomes are reported. Where studies provide project-specific results, we combine these to the sectoral level within a given country. Other studies perform macroeconomic analysis directly, and some of these explore the full range of adaptation needs. These results are shown as histograms over sectors below.
> Adaptation can be measured in different but complementary ways, each offering a distinct lens on its effectiveness. One straightforward measure is the adaptation ratio (AR), which captures the portion of losses that are reduced thanks to adaptation. For example, across the studies synthesised in our analysis, adaptation actions were found to cut impacts by nearly half on average, with especially strong results in projects that provide public goods, technological innovations, or direct financial transfers. This illustrates the practical value of AR as a measure—it tells us how much damage is avoided on the ground, and helps convey adaptation’s tangible role in protecting households, farms, and communities from shocks.
> Estimated adaptation ratios by sector
> Figure 11. Reported adaptation benefit estimates, representing the portion of impacts avoided, grouped by sector and study. Estimates draw from 27 studies.
> 
> 
> Beyond avoided losses, however, policymakers often want to know whether investing in adaptation “pays off” from an economic standpoint. This is where the economic benefit-cost ratio (BCR) comes in. BCR compares the discounted sum of benefits with the discounted sum of costs. In the adaptation studies reviewed, nearly all actions showed a BCR above 1, meaning benefits outweigh costs, with a median value of around 4 to 1. Put simply, every dollar invested in adaptation often delivers four dollars in benefits. For instance, investments in flood defences or resilient infrastructure not only prevent damages during disasters but also improve long-term market access, amplifying the overall return. For economic BCR, both market benefits, such as reduced capital losses, and non-market benefits, such as reduced risk of lost life, are included.
> 
> Estimated economic benefit-cost ratios by sector
> Figure 12. Reported benefit-cost ratios, including market and non-market benefits, grouped by sector and study. Estimates draw from 14 studies.
> 
> 
> A related but distinct measure is the economic internal rate of return (EIRR). Instead of embedding a fixed discount rate like BCR, EIRR identifies the discount rate at which the net present value of benefits minus costs is exactly zero. In practice, this means EIRR tells us how “hard” an investment works relative to opportunity costs of capital. The evidence base suggests that adaptation also performs well when measuring under this metric. The median EIRR across projects in the database is about 25%, which is above the typical thresholds used for public investment decisions. Again, it is important to note that this includes both market and non-market benefits. Some areas, such as disaster-related adaptation, appear to achieve roughly double this median return, showing how well-targeted interventions can deliver large payoffs.
> 
> Estimated economic internal rate of return by sector.
> Figure 13. Reported benefit-cost ratios, including market and non-market benefits, grouped by sector, from World Resources Institute (2025).
> 
> 
> These three measures differ in scope and emphasis. AR focuses solely on avoided losses, while BCR and EIRR explicitly incorporate costs. BCR is tied to a chosen discount rate, whereas EIRR makes the discount rate itself the central outcome. Yet they are also connected: projects with high AR often also yield strong BCRs and EIRRs, and in most cases, high BCRs go hand in hand with high EIRRs. What emerges from the data is a consistent picture: adaptation does not only shield societies from damages; it is also a sound economic investment, generating returns that rival or surpass many conventional development projects.
> It should be noted that many of the underlying results include economic benefits beyond the direct reduction in losses. However, across results where both avoided losses and other aspects of a Triple Dividend from adaptation were reported, we find that the majority of reported adaptation benefits are avoided losses. It is also worth noting that most benefits accrue to individuals, so that fiscal capture of these benefits is challenging.
> We find no evidence that current adaptation options present an unrealistically optimistic view of the returns of the full set of economic adaptation options.
> Importance of co-benefits
> Adaptation has economic benefits beyond avoided losses. The most common co-benefit of adaptation identified in the literature review is the reduction in future increases in borrowing costs due to climatic shocks. According to Cevik et al. (2020), a 1% increase in climate change vulnerability leads to a 3.11% increase in long-term government bond spreads in emerging market economies, while a 1% increase in climate change resilience reduces bond spreads by 0.75%. Early adoption of adaptation strategies has a beneficial impact on the debt-to-GDP ratio because it enables countries to restore their capital stock more quickly after climatic shocks (Catalano et al., 2020). Moreover, adaptation strategies reduce credit rating downgrades, which also has positive fiscal impacts for economies (Klusak et al., 2023).
> 
> Standard Chartered (2023) estimates that co-benefits of adaptation related to GDP boost account for 3.8% of the total benefits of adaptation investment at the national level across 10 market economies in a 1.5°C warming scenario. According to WRI (2025), co-benefits exceed avoided losses in all sectors of the economy when considering not only GDP boost but also social and environmental benefits of adaptation, which include GHG emission reductions, ecosystem services restoration, well-being benefits and health improvements. 
> 
> Adaptation investments can yield substantial returns, particularly when considering the broad co-benefits and opportunities created, although economic returns often surpass financial ones.
> 
> Box 2. Identifying private household resilience to guide ‘easy win’ entry points for public investment in climate resilience: Kenya Case Study
> 
> Awareness of the El Niño-Southern Oscillation (ENSO) is widespread, but the Indian Ocean Dipole (IOD) has only recently gained attention after its severe impacts in East Africa. The IOD is a climate phenomenon marked by shifts in sea surface temperatures between the western and eastern Indian Ocean, classified as negative or positive, which influence rainfall and weather in Africa, Asia, and Australia. In a positive IOD (pIOD), warmer waters in the western Indian Ocean and cooler waters near Indonesia bring heavy rains to East Africa and drought to parts of Australia and Southeast Asia. Moreover, climate change is increasing the frequency of pIOD events, while intensifying hazards which disproportionately affect resource-poor household (REFERENCE).
> The 2019 pIOD drew attention for triggering variable rainfall, cyclones, floods, and Desert Locust outbreaks. Building resilience to pIOD risks requires actors at all levels to understand available options and their effectiveness. Current focus on infrastructure and mortality overlooks wider impacts, risking misallocation of resources. This report draws on World Bank household surveys (2019–2022) to assess hazard impacts and coping mechanisms. The goal is to guide investment priorities by identifying pIOD impacts, evaluating adaptation and coping strategies, and exploring opportunities to strengthen generalized resilience.
> Burden on public budget of complex pIOD risk
> Recovery from the impacts of the 2019 pIOD event East Africa are estimated between $1–9 billion (World Bank, 2020). Vulnerable groups already facing undernutrition, disease, and food price volatility have been hit hardest (Serdeczny et al., 2017). Kenya’s Social Safety Net Programme has been vital, but this analysis finds that government aid spending rose more than forty-fold across survey waves, creating major fiscal pressures (Baeza Breinbauer, forthcoming).
> Spending increases were driven by expanded coverage rather than larger transfers: average benefits per household stayed between 2,100–3,400 Ksh, while coverage rose from <1% to 11% before dipping to 9%. Aid per 100 households peaked at nearly 28,000 Ksh before contracting, reflecting both welfare gains and fiscal risks, including crowding out of health, education, and infrastructure while risking increased public debt.
> Aid distribution showed a strong agricultural bias. Agricultural households received more total and per-household aid than non-agricultural ones, with coverage peaking at 12% versus 3%. This targeted support to rural and food-producing households likely enhanced food security but raises vulnerability concerns and concentrated fiscal strain in one sector.
> Preliminary results of regressions undertaken for a forthcoming paper, reveal mixed targeting (Baeza Breinbauer, forthcoming). Among agricultural workers, farmers, and pastoralists, aid often aligned with food insecurity, loan reliance, and child labour, but wealthier households—those with better housing or electricity—also received disproportionate support. Pastoralists appeared better targeted, with lower risks of elite capture.
> While aid helps shield vulnerable groups, aid alone risks fiscal strain. Strengthening vulnerability-based criteria and institutionalizing spending are key to balancing welfare protection with fiscal sustainability.
> Investment requirements to boost resilience
>  To reduce the fiscal burden of reactive aid and strengthen resilience, investments must address hazard, vulnerability, and response determinants of pIOD risks. The 2019 pIOD exposed Kenyan farmers to multiple hazards. Floods damaged infrastructure, disrupted markets, and reduced yields, while locusts harmed crops and grazing lands, worsened by pesticide shortages and unsafe control practices. Results show exposure is shaped by livelihood, crop choice, and infrastructure. Potato and cassava growers are more flood-prone while banana and kale growers are less exposed to locusts. Electricity access is linked to higher locust exposure in agriculturally active zones—perhaps driven by greater capacity for higher yields that attract locusts.
> Vulnerability depends on local government budgets, preparedness, wealth, and income diversity. Wealth enables recovery and diversification, while weak budgets limit safety net capacity. Social safety nets help but are often underfunded. Pre-existing poverty forces households to buy food when harvests fail. Results demonstrate that farm size is the strongest predictor of earnings, followed by household size and housing quality. Access to fertilizer and credit boosts incomes, but reliance on informal loans often signals distress.
> With regards to how agricultural workers respond, farmers benefit most from land, fertilizer, and formal finance, while pastoralist incomes depend on child labor, credit, and government aid. Wealth indicators improve earnings for both groups. Building resilience requires crop- and livelihood-specific interventions, better credit and input access, stronger safety nets, and sustainable budget frameworks.
> Added benefits of investments
> Investing in climate resilience for Kenyan agricultural households provides benefits beyond fiscal savings, including avoided losses and co-benefits.
> Climate shocks damage infrastructure, disrupt markets, and strain resources, leaving communities with few options: rebuilding, shifting livelihoods, or displacement. Displacement often leads to permanent migration and uncultivated land, worsening desertification and flood risk. Desert Locust outbreaks add further risks, forcing trade-offs between crop destruction and hazardous pesticide spraying.
> Resilience investments reduce hazard exposure and food insecurity while strengthening welfare. Results demonstrate that floods are a consistent driver of hunger across agricultural workers, farmers, and pastoralists. Hunger is also linked to weak assets, poor access to seeds or fertilizer, and reliance on reactive coping mechanisms—credit and child labour. Better housing, electricity, inputs, and food expenditures lower hunger risks. Expanding adaptive credit, safety nets, and resilient inputs reduces reliance on loans and child labour while stabilizing welfare.
> Resilience investments yield three key benefits—(1) avoiding welfare and food security losses, (2) reducing harmful coping mechanisms, and (3) saving fiscally by lowering emergency aid needs—while also mitigating government liabilities from loan defaults and large-scale bailouts.
> Policy recommendations
> Tailored strategies are essential for optimal use of resources. Farmers need improved productivity through land access, inputs, and credit, while pastoralists require stronger financial networks, targeted aid, and stable food access. Based on the evidence presented, the following policy recommendations are proposed to strengthen resilience while ensuring fiscal sustainability:
> • Institutionalize climate-related aid spending within a medium-term expenditure framework to reduce fiscal volatility and ensure predictability.
> • Expand early warning systems and pest surveillance to manage floods, droughts, and locust outbreaks proactively.
> • Strengthen local government fiscal capacity and planning to improve emergency preparedness and reduce reliance on central government bailouts.
> • Invest in resilient infrastructure, including irrigation, flood defences, and rural roads, to mitigate hazard impacts and improve market access.
> • Scale up social safety nets and adaptive credit mechanisms to reduce reliance on harmful coping strategies such as child labour and emergency loans.
> • Improve access to agricultural inputs to boost productivity and resilience of smallholder farmers.
> • Diversify rural livelihoods through education, skills development, and financial inclusion initiatives to reduce vulnerability to single-shock dependence.
> 
> Lessons from adaptation
> The effectiveness of labor reallocation to less weather-exposed economic activities as an adaptation strategy compared to physical adaptation is uncertain.
> 
> Productivity losses from climate shocks may be offset if capital and labor reallocate to less weather-exposed activities (World Bank, 2024a). Nevertheless, the evidence is contradictory. Rode et al. (2022) find that in an RCP8.5 scenario, the global labor disutility costs of climate change in 2099 when there is no adaptation but there is a change in workforce composition toward less weather-exposed economic activities is 1.5%. This value increases by 0.3 percentage points when physical adaptation is adopted, meaning that labor reallocation has a greater impact than physical adaptation strategies in avoiding labor disutility losses.
> 
> Even though in the literature review it was found that rising temperatures is the most common driver of risk studied in macro and fiscal reports, adaptation strategies studied at a national level focus on infrastructure solutions to mitigate capital loss associated with sea-level rise and extreme weather events. Nevertheless, this preventive adaptation is limited as there are more damages (Agrawala et al., 2010). The limited evidence at a national level on cooling systems is not concluding. For firms, the implementation of cooling systems has a medium damage reduction (EDHEC Climate Institute, 2025) and in India the implementation of climate control technology did not reduce absenteeism of manufacturing workers (Somanathan et al., 2021). Nevertheless, other evidence finds that in the DRC cooling systems increase labour productivity (World Bank, 2023d) and technological climate control reduces the effects of climate change (World Bank, 2024a).
> However, evidence from the Sahel region suggests that the difference in avoided GDP losses between the low, medium, and high growth scenarios, which consider a shift in economic activities from agriculture to industry and services sectors, is not as significant as when there is no adaptation. For example, by 2030 in a dry scenario for 2050, avoided losses in Burkina Faso are expected to be 2.7% of GDP in a low-growth scenario, while in a high-growth scenario they are 2.2% of GDP (World Bank, 2022). Moreover, a systematic quantitative literature review by the World Bank (2024a) found that labor reallocation, mostly through migration and off-farm work, reduces climate losses by only 14%. Studies on the constraints of spatial and sectoral reallocation are also very limited.
> 
> 
> Box 3. Nature-based solutions and the potential to achieve development, mitigation, and adaptation: Brazil Case Study
> 
> Brazil faces significant climate risks to agricultural production. Agriculture, forestry, and fishing account for about 6% of Brazil’s GDP. As a major exporter of agricultural products, these impacts are felt globally. Brazil grows 40% of the world’s coffee, and droughts in 2014-16 that reduced yields by 10-15% increased global prices for coffee by 50% (Nestlé 2019). As temperatures climb, the risk of extreme events impacting people and ecosystems increases. By 2050, 93% of Brazil’s population will be at risk of heat waves (Quiggin et al. 2021). If higher temperatures kick off die-back of the Amazon Rainforest, it could cost Brazil 10% of its GDP by 2050 and unleash hundreds of gigatons of CO2 (World Bank Group 2023).
> 
> On the other hand, better ecosystem management has the potential to support strategic goals. By maintaining natural capital and ecosystem services, healthier environments can further economic development. By reducing land use change and reversing the loss of carbon stocks, protected ecosystems can reduce greenhouse emissions. Under the rubric of “nature-based solutions” (NbS), ecosystem management can also provide adaptation benefits. Brazil provides a good case study for the potential of investing in NbS, with a history of success across its agricultural sector and broader economy.
> 
> For example, reduced deforestation can drive a virtuous cycle of agricultural productivity. While deforestation reduces rainfall over large areas and leads to erosion, maintaining forest stands provides benefits outside these areas (Leite-Filho et al. 2021). Higher rainfall results in higher yields for farmers and less risk (Dos Santos et al. 2024). In the southern Amazon, reduced deforestation increases agricultural productivity by up to US$1 billion per year (Leite-Filho et al. 2021).
> 
> Financial incentives can lead to the necessary behaviour changes. When rural credit is made conditional on land use practices that preserve forests, this produces significant reductions in deforestation (Assunção et al. 2019). Brazil invests 0.5% of GDP in financial support, among the highest in Latin America. As a likely result, Brazil has the lowest level of crop vulnerability to droughts in South America (Caucheteux et al. 2024). Brazil also has a long history of market support for coffee farmers in periods of volatility and investing in capital. This has contributed to it having among the highest coffee productivity levels in the world (Sachs et al. 2019).
> 
> The Brazilian Agricultural Policy for Climate Adaptation and Low Carbon Emission (ABC+) serves as a transformative framework that underscores the nation’s commitment to sustainable agriculture through NbS. By promoting practices such as agroforestry, crop-livestock integration, and enhanced soil management, ABC+ seeks to mitigate greenhouse gas emissions while increasing carbon sequestration in agricultural landscapes. This initiative not only addresses climate resilience and adaptation for farmers but also fosters biodiversity and restores ecosystem integrity across Brazil’s diverse biomes. ABC+ represents a strategic investment in the future of agriculture that harmonizes economic growth with environmental stewardship, ultimately leading to long-term benefits for both rural communities and the wider economy.
> 
> Nature-based solutions are also relevant for energy infrastructure, cities, and industry. The Itaipú Dam, the third largest hydropower dam in the world, uses NbS to protect its critical infrastructure and enhance environmental resilience. By planting over 44 million trees and restoring 421 watersheds within the dam’s vicinity, the region has seen improvements in water quality, reduced sedimentation, and improved operational efficiency for the dam in the face of extreme weather (Resilience Shift 2020).
> 
> In Campinas, the Municipal Green Plan (PMV) guides the integration of NbS into urban planning, addressing urban challenges exacerbated by climate change, such as frequent flooding and decreased green space. Notably, the establishment of ecological corridors and linear parks aims to mitigate these issues by enhancing biodiversity, improving air quality, and providing socio-economic benefits like recreation and tourism. A key component of Campinas’ strategy is the regional Connectivity Area, fostering biodiversity conservation and ecosystem service provision across the Metropolitan Region of Campinas. The strategies employed not only align with sustainability goals but serve as scalable models for effective urban governance in other Brazilian cities, illustrating potential pathways for investment that align with economic and environmental priorities (Seleguim et al. 2024).
> 
> NbS also present a significant opportunity to bolster economic resilience and sustainability, particularly amidst the increasing hydrologic volatility driven by climate change. In Brazil, strategic investments in NbS can counteract substantial economic losses associated with drought conditions. For example, NbS applied to the São Paulo Cantareira water supply system are projected to have benefits totaling BRL 443.9 million in avoided losses for the severe 2014–2015 drought. This represents a 28% reduction in the total economic cost of drought for both the industrial and water sectors. Accounting for both increased water supply and reduced social costs associated with carbon sequestration, the net present value of this intervention would be BRL 632 (Ciasca et al. 2023).
> 
> For Ministries of Finance, these examples underscore a viable pathway for not only averting future economic disruptions but also facilitating sustainable, long-term growth that harmonizes environmental restoration with economic development strategies. The integration of NbS could thus be a crucial component in national planning, promoting fiscal stability through enhanced natural capital and reduced reliance on costly engineering-based solutions.
> 
> 
> 
> Adaptation strategies are more effective at reducing climate change damages when GHG emissions are reduced worldwide. Climate damages are highest when neither adaptation nor mitigation strategies are implemented globally. Damages decrease substantially when either adaptation or mitigation strategies are adopted independently, with both approaches yielding approximately equivalent reductions. However, climate damages as a percentage of global GDP are lowest when adaptation and mitigation strategies are combined and implemented simultaneously (Agrawala et al., 2010). 
> 
> There are abundant opportunities for adaptation, decarbonization, and development to effectively work together, by overcoming challenges related to coordination and market dynamics, and thereby crowd in private finance.
> Economy-wide adaptation
> Adaptation investments must be tailored to specific contexts but offer significant benefits on a national scale, supporting national economic objectives and enhancing fiscal health.
> 
> 
