> Box 4. The role of institutions in delivering adaptation outcomes: Rwanda Case Study
> 
> Institutional and governance arrangements are key for delivering adaptation outcomes, including at the macro-economic and fiscal level. Government has a critical role in resolving market failures and supporting enabling factors to deliver adaptation finance and implementation at scale.
> ·   	Adaptation is a process, which combines iterative actions that change over time in response to evolving risks. This requires effective institutional and governance arrangements and clear strategies. There are also barriers to adaptation, including market failures, and there is an important role for government to provide the policy and enabling conditions for adaptation.
> ·   	These institutional and enabling actions are an important form of adaptation in their own right, and are critical for delivering adaptation outcomes. While these issues have been a focus in project and programme level adaptation, there has been less consideration of their important at the macro-economic and fiscal level. This is the focus of this case study.
> ·   	A literature review has identified three important themes for successful macro-economic and fiscal adaptation a) institutional arrangements and coordination b) strategies and mainstreaming and c) enabling factors. Together these can help mobilise adaptation finance, improve effectiveness and scale-up adaptation, though it is more difficult to quantitatively assess these benefits.
> ·   	This case study has then explored these institutional issues with a case study of Rwanda.  This country has one of the most advanced climate policy landscapes in the world. The case study has undertaken a detailed institutional review using social network mapping. It then conducted interviews to provide insights on what the most important factors have been.
> 
> ·   	This revealed that the institutional arrangements and coordination, with responsibilities and mandates extending beyond the Ministry of Environment across government, had been a key factor in driving adaptation.
> ·   	The interviews also reinforced the role of national strategies, which have driven programming and prioritisation, but perhaps more critically, the integration (mainstreaming) of adaptation by the Ministry of Finance and Economic Planning into national development planning strategies (5 year plans) rather than as stand-alone plans.
> ·   	There was also a high level of integration in systems, with the integration of climate in economic and fiscal planning, including medium-term expenditure planning, budgeting including climate budget tagging, and macroeconomic and fiscal analysis.
> ·   	The mapping found that Rwanda has introduced nearly all the enabling factors that are identified in the literature, e.g., with a country platform, green taxonomy, programmatic adaptation investment planning, risk disclosure analysis. Of these, one of the most impactful was the set-up of the Green Fund, which was identified as being extremely effective as a country coordination platform and mobilised large volumes of adaptation finance (especially concessional finance). However, there was evidence of many innovative aspects, with a private sector climate facility, and a shift to a range of instruments and initiatives (investment loans and policy based loans, on-lending, blended finance, equity and guarantees, green bonds).
> ·   	When asked what had catalysed all of this – the high level buy-in was identified. Importantly, there was also a recognition across government that climate change was a key issue for Rwanda and doing nothing was not an option. This had informed by cost of inaction studies, but also recent climate shocks, notably the 2023 landslides that had large macroeconomic and fiscal impacts. The capacity building and strengthening that had been provided to help Rwanda to this was also acknowledged as being an important factor.
> 
> 
> Adaptation costs
> Understanding the cost dynamics of adaptation investments involves distinguishing between capital expenditures (CapEx) and operational expenditures (OpEx). While CapEx refers to upfront investment costs, OpEx embodies the ongoing operational maintenance required to maximize lifespan and efficacy. 
> 
> Non-monetary costs, such as social and cultural impacts, must be acknowledged and integrated to present a holistic evaluation of adaptation strategies. Of the 28 studies reviewed, only 4 included residual damages after implementing adaptation strategies as a cost in addition to financial costs. Moreover, the quantification of labor reallocation, both as an adaptation strategy and as a consequence of adaptation, is limited. This gap is significant given that labour reallocation represents a common household adaptation strategy (World Bank, 2024a).
> 
> Adaptation costs are estimated at US$215 billion to US$387 billion for 2030 for developing countries (UNEP, 2023). Adaptation costs are estimated at US$320 to 365 billion/year by 2035 for developing countries (UNEP, 2025). Adaptation costs are expected to triple for the median country by 2035 (Chapagain et al. 2020). While low-income and lower-middle income countries have lower absolute adaptation costs than upper-middle and high-income countries, their adaptation finance needs constitute a higher share of GDP (WRI, 2025). Agrawala et al. (2010) finds the same results when comparing Sub-Saharan Africa and India/Indian subcontinent in comparison to China and the USA.
> 
> Adaptation requires public planning. Since the most effective adaptation strategies are public goods, their associated costs cannot be assumed by individual firms or households. The adaptation ratio of public goods such as infrastructure, effective health systems and piped water is 0.64, which is the highest out of all the strategies considered (World Bank, 2024a). Furthermore, adaptation costs are projected to rise as a percentage of GDP over time, especially for capital-intensive investments, making early investments with long-term benefits a crucial component of adaptation strategies (Agrawala et al., 2010).
> 
> Adaptation costs are lower when there is more mitigation at a worldwide level. By 2050, adaptation costs for developing countries will be 0.27-0.89% of GDP in a high-emission scenario, while in a low-emission scenario they will be 0.11-0.35% of GDP (Chapagain et al., 2020). By 2100, adaptation costs in the below 2°C scenario represent less than 1% of global GDP, whereas in a 3°C scenario they reach 4% (World Economic Forum, 2024).
> 
> Research gaps
> Evidence on adaptation is global, but large countries (US, China, India, Brazil) have vastly more estimates.
> 
> Three quarters of the available evidence is from ex-ante studies. These likely miss both important costs and benefits.
> 
> 
> Availability of adaptation estimates and sources
> 
> Figure 15. Global distribution of available adaptation estimates. Top: The number of estimates related to adaptation benefits, by country. For estimates applied uniformly to many countries, the estimate count is evenly divided across countries. Bottom: The number of sources providing country-specific values, by country.
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> Most studies select adaptation strategies based on maximizing the discounted sum of net benefits. Nevertheless, given that Ministries of Finance have limited resources, the impact of different levels of adaptation strategies on macroeconomic variables could be studied in greater depth, as this has only been addressed in a few of the studies reviewed. 
> 
> Moreover, only one study (Agrawala et al., 2010) examined the difference between early stock capital adaptation and reactive flow capital adaptation. The timing of adaptation investments and which types of adaptation should be prioritized require further study.
> 
> Adaptation costs should consider economic costs that impact welfare, such as the quantification of labor reallocation costs, which is a common adaptation strategy adopted by households.
> 
> The methodology used to measure adaptation benefits on firms' net asset value in the literature reviewed (EDHEC Climate Institute, 2025) is mostly descriptive, and the climate change scenarios are not clearly defined. The financial benefits of adaptation for firms should be calculated using more rigorous methods.
> 
> Adaptation strategies related to fiscal adjustments, changes in household savings, and firm investments need to be studied in greater detail. Bakkensen et al. (2018) provide a useful example of how to incorporate these macroeconomic adaptation strategies into climate change adaptation analysis. According to the literature reviewed, adaptation studies have mostly focused on physical adaptation in stock and flow capital.
> 
> Macroeconomic frictions in implementing adaptation strategies, such as labor market frictions, require further study to guide MoFs toward more efficient adaptation.
>  
> Chapter 4. A framework for investment
> Adaptation is a crucial component of addressing the challenges of climate change. While mitigation efforts aim to reduce greenhouse gas emissions, adaptation focuses on minimizing the vulnerabilities and risks associated with climate impacts. Adaptation and mitigation work together, allowing us to confront the immediate and evolving challenges posed by a warming world.
> 
> There are many complementary motivations for investing in adaptation. Adaptation efforts are critical not only for safeguarding human health, infrastructure, and economic stability, but also for protecting natural capital, which serves a necessary input for economic growth. These initiatives are invariably context-specific, due to the diverse array of local environmental, social, and economic conditions. Therefore, the design and implementation of adaptation measures must be tailored to the unique needs and capacities of each community.
> 
> Importantly, not all adaptation measures require significant financial investment. Much can be gained by embedding adaptation strategies into existing decision-making processes. By integrating climate considerations into urban planning, infrastructure development, and other sectors, we can improve resilience at a fraction of the cost. For instance, the upcoming IDB adaptation report highlights the significance of incorporating adaptive planning in policymaking, which can yield substantial dividends by preemptively addressing risks.
> 
> It is crucial to avoid path-dependencies or “lock-ins” when considering adaptation measures. Decisions made today have long-term implications for future risk levels. Where and how we choose to build infrastructure will shape vulnerability and resilience patterns for decades. Hence, proactive and strategic planning is essential to avoid costly missteps and to enhance adaptive capacity.
> 
> From an economic standpoint, investing in adaptation is not merely a defensive strategy but is supported by a robust economic rationale. The costs of inaction or delayed response are far greater than proactive adaptation investments. This economic perspective is rooted in a compelling body of evidence and is underpinned by the quantitative insights referenced throughout this report. By acting decisively now, we not only reduce future risks but also catalyze economic benefits, enhance societal well-being, and pave the way for sustainable development.
> The economic case for adaptation
> Investing in climate adaptation is a strategic economic decision with far-reaching implications. A robust economic case for adaptation can be articulated through various lenses, including co-benefits, immediate and future benefits, as well as challenges and opportunities for investment.
> 
> One of the compelling aspects of adaptation is its potential for co-benefits. These spillover effects extend beyond mere climate resilience and encompass emissions reduction, ecological restoration, and social equity. Adaptation has a Triple Resilience Dividend, and can yield returns that improve environmental health, enhance community well-being, and bolster economic stability (World Resources Institute 2025).
> 
> However, not all co-benefits can be easily quantified or monetized. Many advantages, such as improved biodiversity and human health, provide intrinsic value that goes beyond financial metrics but are nonetheless vital for sustainable development. Recognizing these broader benefits can help justify investment in adaptation initiatives that might not produce immediate or direct financial returns yet contribute to long-term societal resilience.
> 
> The economic returns on adaptation investments can be realized in both the short and long term. Immediate benefits may include avoided damages from climate-related disasters, such as flooding or heatwaves, which can have direct economic implications. For example, proactive investments in protective infrastructure can significantly reduce the economic burden associated with disaster response and recovery.
> 
> We show above that long-term benefits outweigh the upfront costs of adaptation. By fostering resilience today, we can reduce future vulnerabilities and the associated economic risk. As empirical evidence shows, strategic investments in adaptation yield positive net present values, protecting economies from the escalating costs of climate change and positioning nations for sustainable growth.
> 
> Despite the clear economic rationale for adaptation, several challenges and barriers continue to impede private and public investment. These include coordination challenges across sectors, a lack of understanding regarding the quantification of risks, and insufficient metrics to measure the impacts of adaptation efforts. Moreover, many adaptation interventions may not generate immediate financial returns, leading to hesitance from decision-makers who are focused on short-term economic indicators.
> 
> To overcome these barriers, it is essential to enhance collaboration between economic actors, improve risk assessment methodologies, and develop clear frameworks for measuring the economic benefits of adaptation. Ministries of Finance have an important role in driving this work, and through it better advocating for and allocating resources toward adaptation initiatives.
> 
> Despite these challenges, there are several “low-hanging fruits” or low-regret adaptation strategies that offer clear, immediate economic benefits while minimizing risks. These include measures such as disaster preparedness, urban greening initiatives, and resilient agricultural practices. Many of these strategies require minimal upfront investment while producing tangible benefits such as increased agricultural productivity, improved air and water quality, and strengthened community resilience.
> 
> Focusing on these low-regret options not only boosts immediate resilience but also lays the groundwork for more comprehensive and ambitious adaptation efforts in the future. By prioritising these strategies, Ministries of Finance and other economic actors can catalyse momentum toward a more resilient and sustainable economy, showcasing the transformative potential of strategic adaptation investments.
> Closing adaptation gaps
> Closing existing adaptation gaps requires a multi-faceted approach informed by good governance, policy-first frameworks, effective collaboration between public and private sectors, and a commitment to robust decision-making under forward-looking considerations.
> 
> Effective governance is crucial for driving successful adaptation initiatives. A policy-first approach ensures that adaptation measures are systematically integrated into national strategies and local practices (see upcoming IADB report on adaptation). This entails creating an enabling environment for sustainable investment, establishing clear leadership structures, and fostering stakeholder engagement to ensure that adaptation efforts are responsive to local needs and priorities.
> 
> Balancing the roles of public and private sectors is essential in addressing adaptation gaps effectively (Watkiss & England 2025). Public institutions carry the responsibility of creating regulatory frameworks and providing infrastructural support, while private entities contribute innovation, investment, and efficiency to adaptation efforts.
> 
> Collaboration is key; by leveraging the strengths of both sectors, we can maximize resources, mitigate risks, and drive down adaptation costs. Public-private partnerships can amplify the impact of investments, enhance knowledge-sharing, and foster community engagement to build a more resilient future.
> 
> Numerous tools and frameworks are available to assist governments and financial institutions in assessing and managing physical climate risks and adaptation strategies. A notable resource is “How Finance Ministries Can Assess and Manage Physical Climate Risks and Adaptation,” published earlier this year. This report provides insights and guidelines on integrating climate risk assessment within fiscal policy and decision-making processes, highlighting existing methodologies that can be employed to ensure sustainable adaptation investments.
> 
> Robust decision-making provides a broad framework for approaching some of the key issues in long-term adaptation investment planning. This approach involves evaluating a diverse range of potential outcomes, accounting for uncertainties, and developing strategies that remain effective under varying conditions. By employing scenario analysis, stress testing, and other forward-looking methodologies, decision-makers can make informed investments that not only enhance resilience but also foster economic growth.
> Guidance for Ministries of Finance
> Sound economic decisions around adaptation investments start by developing a long-term vision.
> 
> Hazard Identification and Economic Impact Assessment: Begin by systematically identifying potential climate hazards and quantifying their economic impacts across multiple time horizons—2030, 2040, 2050, and 2075. Utilize tools like those described in “How Finance Ministries Can Assess and Manage Physical Climate Risks and Adaptation” to develop specific sectoral estimates. This forward-looking approach allows for a comprehensive understanding of potential future risks and sets the stage for proactive rather than reactive decision-making.
> 
> Identification and Evaluation of Adaptation Options: Classify adaptation strategies into various options—such as infrastructure updates, policy reforms, or community-based adaptations. For each option, assess its applicability to your region, upfront and ongoing costs, potential to mitigate damages, associated co-benefits (e.g., job creation, ecosystem services), and any dependencies (e.g., technological availability, regulatory alignment, or prior adaptation actions).
> 
> Backcasting to Define the Adaptation Trajectory: Map the desired adaptation landscape for 2075, defining the strategic objectives. Work backward to outline the necessary investments for each preceding time period—2050, 2040, and 2030. Begin each stage by identifying regional investments with a high potential benefit-to-cost ratio. Move forward investments identified in a future period when feasible, ensuring alignment and continuity. This step integrates long-term vision with actionable short-term goals. By articulating a coherent trajectory, it maintains flexibility while ensuring that intermediate investments are strategically aligned with long-term objectives, minimizing the risk of stranded assets and ensuring sustained progress.
> 
> Each step above incorporates economic consideration by using estimates and assessments to prioritize high-impact areas, leveraging cost-benefit analysis for adaptation options, and ensuring alignment of short-term expenditures with long-term economic stability and resilience. This structured approach allows for dynamic adjustments as new data and technologies emerge, enhancing both robustness and adaptability.
> 
> As Box 5 shows, enshrining these ideas in nation-wide plans can also help unlock new international funding, including concessionary finance, technical assistance, and capacity building.
> 
> Box 5. Long-Term Risks Require Long-Term Planning: Bangladesh Case Study
>  
> Bangladesh has been identified as one of the countries with the greatest risks from climate change, with a vulnerable population exposed to multiple extreme hazards, including heat waves, rising sea-levels, and frequent flooding. In particular, Bangladesh faces higher rainfall in its wet months, potentially quadrupling the risk of extreme monsoon driving flooding by mid-century (Fahad et al. 2023, Kamruzzaman et al. 2024). These risks are already manifesting, with the worst floods in more than 100 years occurring in 2022. Studies have highlighted the disproportionate impact on women and the elderly, with potential for food insecurity and health risks (Hazrana et al. 2025). The long-term impacts of past disasters, like the 1970 cyclone, demonstrate lasting health, education, and economic adversities (Eskander & Barbier 2022).
>  
> Like many developing countries, Bangladesh aims to integrate the multiple demands of climate adaptation, economic development, and climate mitigation. Climate resilient development pathways (CRDP) has emerged as a central paradigm for doing this. CRDP recognises the role of forward-looking planning of actions when engaging in adaptation to risks that will continue evolving for decades.
>  
> Bangladesh has developed a state-of-the-art example of CRDP in the Bangladesh Delta Plan 2100 (BDP 2100). BDP 2100 is a comprehensive, multi-hazard and multi-sector plan, with the mission to “ensure long-term water and food security, economic growth, and environmental sustainability while effectively reducing vulnerability to natural disasters and building resilience to climate change and other delta challenges through robust, adaptive, and integrated strategies, and equitable water governance” (Arfanuzzaman 2024). A few of the adaptation strategies are mapped below to show key decision-points and dependences. If fully implemented, BDP 2100 is expected to eliminate a 5.6% decline in GDP that Bangladesh would otherwise experience (World Bank 2021).
> 
> More broadly, Bangladesh is notable in its approach to integrating climate adaptation and economic development. It has centered the national-level planning processes itself, producing significant documents such as the National Action Plan for Clean Cooking (2019), Sustainable Finance Policy (2020), National Strategy on Internal Displacement Management (2021), National Adaptation Plan (2022), Sector Action Plan on Environment and Climate Change (2023), and Mujib Climate Prosperity Plan (2024).
>  
> Together, the BDP 2100, National Adaptation Plan, and Mujib Climate Prosperity Plan lay out a need for $357.7 billion in funding (Arfanuzzaman 2024). This is significantly greater than what is possible for the Bangladesh government alone, which invests $4.7 billion in development, with about 50% of that provided by international aid (EDC 2020). Rather than focusing on the shortfall, Bangladesh has used its planning process as a key component of its effective approach to securing international finance. For example, this likely contributed to their receiving the largest disbursements from the UNFCCC Adaptation Fund in South Asia (Arfanuzzaman 2024).
>  
> Bangladesh’s approach to planning offers elements of a blueprint to follow their success. First, Bangladesh uses advanced, internationally recognised frameworks, like CRDP, to construct integrated perspectives. They design public-private partnerships, with project selection processes and government co-funding, as part of their plans (EDC 2020). And finally, they have created a dedicated adaptation fund, the Bangladesh Climate Change Resilience Fund, to support these projects. As an integrated approach of conceptualisation, engagement, funding, and implementation, Bangladesh’s planning process itself is a top example of adaptation in action.
